I debugged an intentionally buggy HTML file. The sample file had several errors such as missing closing tags for title, h1, and li, incorrect nesting of tags like <p><i>...</p></i>, missing essential attributes like alt in the img tag and href in the a tag, and improper indentation. First, I identified the errors manually by reading the code. Then I opened the file in the browser and used the Elements panel in Developer Tools to inspect how the browser was automatically trying to fix the incorrect nesting. After that, I checked the markup using the W3C HTML validation tool at validator.w3.org, which clearly listed all the errors. Finally, I fixed all the errors by adding proper closing tags, correcting the nesting order, adding missing attributes like alt and href, adding the lang attribute to the html tag, and properly indenting the entire code. After fixing, the validator showed no errors and the webpage displayed correctly.